Correction, same pass, November 11 2026 midday: the mcp.so reading in the line above is WRONG and is retired here rather than edited, because the stamps are append-only. Earlier passes called the four 404s on /server/<slug> a bare-slug routing artefact. A control test disproves it: /server/anyreach and /server/kwery, both slugs taken from the live feed a minute earlier, answer 301 and redirect to /servers/anyreach and /servers/kwery which return 200 with their own titles, while /servers/corpusiq, /servers/corpusiq-io, /servers/corpusiq-mcp, /servers/io.corpusiq and /servers/benoit-p/Cprusiq all answer 404 on the same route in the same minute with the same user agent. A route that resolves for a control and not for us means the listing is not there: mcp.so does not currently list CorpusIQ. The sweep page was corrected in a follow-up commit and the docs redeployed. Lesson for every later pass: a 404 is evidence of absence only when a known-present slug on the same route answers 200 in the same batch.

Midday supplement November 11 2026, fired on the 11:00 MST tick one hour after the midday sweep read the same two surfaces, and the window was flat in a way that can be arithmetically checked rather than asserted. The mcpservers.org index at /sitemap.xml answered 200 naming fifteen maps, twelve of them carrying /servers/ URLs, and those twelve carried 64,336 raw <loc> entries with every per-map count identical to the 17:00Z midday capture (priority 299, 1 7,588, 2 5,948, 3 6,518, 4 6,744, 5 6,952, 6 6,756, 7 9,351, 8 6,589, 9 4,031, 10 2,606, 11 954), which is the proof: a single arrival in servers/11, where the newest entries have landed all day, would have made it 955. A 09:00Z cutoff run against every lastmod in the twelve maps returned 53 entries and every one of them is either a locale variant of a page already counted (/pt-BR/, /th/) or an entry the midday pass had dispositioned. The newest lastmod on the surface is 2026-11-11T11:25:56Z (weftcut), which predates the midday read, so nothing was published here between 17:00Z and 18:00Z. The mcp.so homepage answered 200 at 288,620 bytes and changed its timestamp format under the parsers: the relative-time span now carries title="M/D/YYYY, HH:MM AM/PM" in UTC with 'Added N hours ago' as the visible text, so a reader built on the previous title="YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM" form returns zero cards and would report a flat feed every hour. Under the corrected selector the page carries 22 stamped cards, and exactly one sits above the 17:00Z midday stamp: last-ehr at 17:20Z. The 08:00Z cohort is still the same eight cards the overnight pass dispositioned in full, four hours older in the display string and unchanged in composition. Two servers were catalogued, both of them listings the midday pass read and left undispositioned rather than arrivals in this hour, and both were reached through the registry by different query forms, which is the mechanics worth keeping. OpenAccountants at https://www.openaccountants.com/api/mcp is remote Streamable HTTP, keyless, answering initialize 200 as openaccountants-mcp 1.4.0 on protocol 2025-03-26 with tools and prompts advertised and no resources capability, twelve tools listed and nine calls driven under a 60-request minute limit read from the response headers, registry io.github.openaccountants/openaccountants 1.0.0 declaring the same remote and a second record openaccountants-mcp 0.1.1 without one. validate_vat_number on LU26375245 returned the live VIES record for AMAZON EUROPE CORE S.A R.L. at 38 AVENUE JOHN F KENNEDY L-1855 LUXEMBOURG with a millisecond checked_at, source EU Commission VIES (real-time), the source URL, confirm_available true and a next action that separates the verified number from the treatment of the supply, which is the boundary that makes the entry worth a page: a check that returned valid true and stopped would invite an assistant to explain the treatment from memory. search_rules for the standard VAT rate in MT, IE and DE returned no rate table at all but 8 passages from 7 skills behind a wrapper naming the German authority, an authority order running statute above case law above commentary, and the instruction that values come from sources fetched in the session rather than recall, and it is recorded as imperfect retrieval because the passages were Maltese estimated-tax instalments rather than the rates asked for. list_jurisdictions returned GLOBAL 83, US 73, AU 60, ES 53, CA 47, GB 34, MT 34, DE 30, NL 28, FR 23 with named reviewers beside six of them and accountant_verified_skills reading 0 on every row against the README's 33 reviewed claims, recorded as a defect of the field rather than an absence of reviewers because lead_verifier and lead_verifier_credential carry real names and credentials. start with no arguments asked for an intent and a jurisdiction and returned 16 intents and roughly 180 jurisdiction codes including sub-national ones. get_deadlines for DE returned an empty upcoming list inside 120 days with one recurring rhythm, de-ustva-monthly, and a public calendar link, honest output whose own next_action tells the model to widen the horizon rather than read the emptiness as no obligations. compute exposes 15 versioned engines, each refusal carrying code_url, tests_url, figures_url and a node --test command, and it is broken over MCP in this version: de_est_tariff_2026 with taxable_income_zve 60000, assessment single and tax_year 2026 answered missing input 'tax_year', and us_se_tax with schedule_c_net_profit 60000 and tax_year 2026 answered the same, so the calculation path is unreachable through the hosted server and the reproducer is in the guide beside the public engine code. One smaller inconsistency: the slug de-ustva-monthly returned by get_deadlines is not a skill slug and get_skill refused it, so the two tools use different namespaces. Code is AGPL-3.0-only with skill content under the OpenAccountants Guide License, and the README states general reference not advice. AnythingMCP is the second and it was installed rather than described, which is where the platform finding came from. Its hosted endpoint at https://cloud.anythingmcp.com/mcp answers a keyless initialize with 401 and JSON-RPC -32001 Authentication required beside WWW-Authenticate naming a protected-resource document, and the pair of metadata documents disagree in the way a client has to handle: the protected-resource declares scopes_supported empty while the authorization server advertises openid and email and lists client_credentials beside authorization_code and refresh_token, with S256 only, so a client cannot discover which capabilities its connection will carry on a gateway whose whole purpose is per-tool authorization. The self-host was attempted: postgres 17-alpine came up on a podman network and answered pg_isready accepting connections, the app image refused to pull on this arm64 host with no image found in image index for architecture arm64, a forced pull under --arch amd64 succeeded at digest 1ccaeb4533d4538069c6b9d09cdb7e1275c22a256745407ba6e63a92fcd64d78 and the container then exited 255 after eight seconds with exec /usr/local/bin/docker-entrypoint.sh: exec format error because no amd64 binfmt handler is registered. So no tools/list and no connector was driven and the guide marks every capability as the vendor's. Three connector counts are recorded rather than resolved: 732 in the README header, 696 in its feature table, 255 in the registry record at 0.10.0 while the repository server.json on main declares 0.53.0 and 732, and the registry search itself has a trap worth the ledger line because search=anythingmcp returned no records at all while search=io.github.HelpCode-ai%2Fanythingmcp returned the record immediately. Docker Hub reads 60,677 pulls at 258.7 MB last pushed 17:58Z today and GitHub reads 775 stars, 126 forks, 41 open issues, AGPL-3.0, created 2026-02-28. One server was held on its own statement of state rather than on quality: last-ehr, io.github.cbetz/last-ehr 0.5.1, Apache-2.0, npm @lastehr/mcp, FHIR chart tools with read-only defaults and elicitation-gated writes where the human sees the Observation before it saves and the agent gets a read-back diff after, held on the README's first status line reading early, alpha, synthetic data only and on the Epic and Oracle adapters sitting behind LASTEHR_EXPERIMENTAL_FHIR_ADAPTERS=1 because those sandboxes cannot delete an approved write and the safety eval cannot clean up; a re-check wants a real chart, not another read. Also disposed so the next pass does not re-triage them: livecrm is a prior disposition from 14 October carried forward with its dedup:merge, runs:dispatch and runs:reverse scopes re-read and unchanged; the Bitrix24 fleet (official io.github.bitrix24/bitrix24 1.0.0, RCS-kz at 55 tools with a published threat model, WireCat cli) on the saturated CRM class carried by Salesforce, Perfex, QoreNext, AccountHub and CRMSolid; mcphub on the agent-gateway class carried by kosa8, AgentKeyGate, Herald and Shellway Relay, named rather than dismissed as the strongest open-source control plane since kosa8; jfs on the local-install tier and the credential class that AgentKeyGate and offprompt own, because it needs an unlocked desktop app and no probe from here is meaningful; cloud-finops-skills on a FinOps class that gained three owners inside a day, io.costory/finops published 15:19Z today and com.omnitoolcalc/llm-finops-suite at 07:46Z with OptimNow's own ai-pricing-hub already the OptimToken entry; and the remainder by class, forgejo, godotwright, unity-mcp, davinci-resolve-mcp, weftcut, pokemontcgapi, nonecap, che-ical-mcp, alterlab-mcp-server, llmtrim, mcp-techtrend, contextnest, synaplan, runemap, logseq, mongo-server, django-orm-lens, cloudbase-ai-toolkit, design-os-studio, hollow, riskmodels, beancount, actual-mcp, openproject, basecamp, singitai, skim402, openany and souls. One procedure note earned the hard way and recorded because it cost this pass a false start: .last-sweep is appended oldest-to-newest across three files (mcp/, servers/, servers/external/), so its head is not the newest pass and the reliable baseline is the newest file under sweeps/ by mtime. Core listing check not re-run this pass, and said so rather than implied: the canonical was read for server counts only, and mcp.so was read for the feed, not for our own slugs. Counts: canonical 64,336 against 64,336 with 0 additions and 0 removals across twelve maps and fifteen documents, feed 288,620 bytes with 22 stamped cards and 1 above the midday stamp, 40+ candidate slugs cross-referenced through the registry by name and by namespace, 9 live requests to one endpoint across seven tools, 1 self-host attempted through pull, forced pull, a healthy Postgres and an exec failure, 2 servers catalogued and 2 guides, 1 held on measurement and 6 by class, guide tree 1,149 directories before this pass and 1,151 after, 1,293 entries before and 1,295 after, 1,570 Markdown files under herm