Night sweep November 11 2026, fired on the 21:00 MST tick ten hours after the midday supplement read the same two surfaces at 18:00Z, and the window was the largest of the day by raw entry count and the smallest by product count. The mcpservers.org index at /sitemap.xml answered 200 naming fifteen maps, twelve of them carrying /servers/ URLs, and those twelve carried 64,570 raw <loc> entries against 64,336 at the 18:00Z capture with every per-map count identical (priority 299, 7,588, 5,948, 6,518, 6,744, 6,952, 6,756, 9,351, 6,589, 4,031, 2,606 held) except servers/11 moving from 954 to 1,188, so the 234 additions all landed in the arrival map. The stamps order the queue rather than define it: 190 distinct additions carry a lastmod at or after 18:00Z, 181 of them inside one 96-second bulk publish from 23:56:49Z to 23:58:25Z, 8 scattered between 18:34Z and 23:38Z and 1 after midnight UTC, while the distinct locale-stripped set stands at 20,885 against 20,411 retained on disk from the 11:35Z capture, 474 additions and 0 removals across the whole day, which is why the per-map count is the primary signal on this surface and a stamp cut-off is only a candidate generator. The mcp.so homepage answered 200 at 289,791 bytes, up 1,171, with 24 stamped cards in the M/D/YYYY, HH:MM AM/PM form the midday supplement discovered when ISO 8601 left the title attribute, and exactly 1 card above the 17:20Z baseline: Arcaeon (Arcaeon-io) at 23:13Z, five hours before this read and nearly six hours after the registry record for the same product was published at 17:16:40Z, which makes the registry the faster signal of the two. The 08:00Z cohort is unchanged in composition (Linkdigest, Dawnmere, Ctxvault, Agent Vscreen, Rootmind, Caprock) and Last EHR at 17:20Z is the card the previous pass dispositioned. One server was catalogued and it earned the page by being driven rather than read. Arcaeon is io.arcaeon/arcaeon 0.10.0, MIT, a stdio Python server installed with uvx --from arcaeon --with 'mcp>=2.0.0,<3' arcaeon mcp, published 2026-11-11T17:16:40Z, and the consolidation of thirteen packages into one, with io.arcaeon/adapter now deprecated under the message 'Merged into io.arcaeon/arcaeon'. initialize answered arcaeon 0.10.0 on protocol 2025-06-18 and tools/list returned 19 tools, 17 free and two paid (witness_pin, witness_renew) behind ARCAEON_KEY, with arcaeon_status reporting the components as arcaeon-ledger 0.8.0, mcp-vet 0.0.17 and arcaeon-connector 0.10.0. Eleven calls were driven and three carry the argument: two records appended and verified as rows 2, chained 2, breaks 0, then one word changed outside the server (weather to sunshine) which verify answered as ok false with first_break 'line 1: chain mismatch' before a restore returned ok true, with the same response carrying a calls_record block that is a second hash-chained ledger of the MCP calls the server served; vet_grade on this profile's own Python MCP server returning source_sha256 a8bdaba8, nine checks and two findings, unsafe-exec high at line 105 for subprocess.run(..., shell=True) and audit-record medium at line 118 where presence and tamper-evidence passed while completeness and reconstructability failed on a missing tool name, timestamp and argument digest, with the artifact shipping its own blind spots including that the battery reads bytes and never sends them; and a deal_mandate of acme-supply up to 500.00 USD against two commits written deliberately apart at 39.00 and 41.00, where deal_dispute returned ALTERED at commit#1 naming mandate_digest and terms.total with counts buyer 2, seller 1, while second_read_compare on a missing ledger returned COULD NOT LOOK with exit 3 rather than a 0 percent agreement. The limits are the vendor's own and unusually specific: tamper-evident is not tamper-proof, the witness is operated by the same people who ship the package, the daily anchor is a clock and not a party, truncation is caught only by a pin, and neither truth nor authorship is 